olivia: Quick QA — I'm about to land a Terraform change that moves inference-gateway svc port from 8080 -> 9090 and switches target type to ip (NLB passthrough). PR: https://github.com/redwood/inference/pull/4828. Any immediate red flags on healthchecks / probes? Need to merge today.

vikram: Quick q — are you switching Service type to NodePort or staying ClusterIP with annotation target-type=ip?

olivia: TF snippet:
```hcl
resource "kubernetes_service" "inference_gateway" {
  metadata {
    name = "inference-gateway"
    annotations = {
      "service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-type" = "nlb"
      "service.beta.kubernetes.io/aws-load-balancer-target-type" = "ip"
    }
  }
  spec {
    port { port = 9090; target_port = 9090; protocol = "TCP" }
    selector = { app = "inference" }
    type = "LoadBalancer"
  }
}
```
Question: should target_port be containerPort (9090) or the nodePort? The pod readiness probe uses localhost:9090.

jamie: If target-type=ip then NLB will send to pod IP:targetPort — so target_port must match containerPort (9090). If you used instance target-type then healthcheck hits NodePort on nodes.

sre-bot: FYI: Known gotcha — Gateway API TCPRoute + TLS passthrough can drop SNI if svc targetPort != containerPort. See internal runbook: https://redwood.internal/runbooks/nlb-passthrough

mike: Also set spec.externalTrafficPolicy = "Local" so source IP preserved (and so healthchecks see a local node). Otherwise we saw sticky source issues during canary.

vikram: +1. Also ensure your readiness probe's host is 0.0.0.0 (not localhost) if you expect node-level health to reach it via pod IP.

olivia: Got it. I'll update PR to set target_port=9090, add externalTrafficPolicy Local, and include a canary rollout note. Thanks :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: Canary rollout scheduled at 15:30 UTC. Watching healthchecks for 30m.

olivia: merging after tests pass. ping @jamie @vikram if anything breaks.
